Project :02 


This project has stronger UX potential than it first appears because the value is not the version control itself, but how users find, understand, and manage large amounts of code efficiently. A senior UX designer would frame it around improving information architecture, search, discoverability, and decision-making rather than database storage.

Overview

Designed the user experience for an internal Version Control System that enabled teams to store, organize, search, and retrieve source code files along with associated metadata. The project focused on improving information discoverability, reducing search time, and simplifying code management workflows for technical users.

Problem

As the repository grew, locating the correct version of a code file became increasingly time-consuming. Users relied on multiple search attempts, inconsistent naming conventions, and manually reviewing metadata to identify the required file.

Although the backend stored code and related information effectively, the experience of finding and managing files lacked clarity, resulting in slower workflows and reduced productivity.

Goal

Design an experience that enables users to:

Find code files quickly using multiple search methods.
Filter large repositories with minimal effort.
View important metadata before opening a file.
Reduce time spent locating the correct version.
Improve confidence when selecting code for development or review.
Users

Primary users included:

Software Developers
QA Engineers
Technical Leads

These users regularly searched for existing code, reviewed previous versions, and referenced project metadata during development and maintenance activities.

Key UX Challenges
Large repositories made code difficult to locate.
Search results often contained too many similar files.
Important metadata was hidden until after opening a file.
Users had limited ways to narrow search results.
File identification relied heavily on naming conventions.
Design Process
Understanding User Workflows

Mapped how developers searched for code during daily tasks, identifying friction points from initiating a search to selecting the correct file.

Identifying Pain Points

Observed recurring challenges, including:

Repeated searches before finding the correct file.
Difficulty distinguishing similar versions.
Excessive navigation between search results and file details.
Time spent manually verifying metadata.
Information Architecture

Structured the repository around users' mental models rather than database organization.

Organized content into:

Search
Filters
File List
Metadata
Version History

This improved navigation and reduced the effort required to locate relevant files.

Design Decisions
Intelligent Search Experience

Designed a search interface that supported searching by:

File name
Project
Module
Certificate date
Metadata
Keywords

This allowed users to locate files through multiple paths instead of relying solely on exact file names.

Advanced Filtering

Introduced contextual filters that enabled users to progressively narrow large result sets without restarting their search.

Filters included:

Project
Date
Module
Version
File Type

This reduced the number of irrelevant search results and shortened the decision-making process.

Metadata-First Design

Displayed key metadata directly within search results, allowing users to compare files before opening them.

Visible information included:

Version
Certificate Date
Project Name
Last Updated
Author (if applicable)

This minimized unnecessary navigation and improved selection confidence.

Improved Result Hierarchy

Designed search results with clear visual hierarchy so users could quickly scan file names, metadata, and version information.

Important information was prioritized while secondary details remained accessible without overwhelming the interface.

Faster File Discovery

Reduced interaction steps by allowing users to search, filter, preview metadata, and access files from a single workflow, minimizing context switching.

UX Principles Applied
Information Architecture
Recognition Over Recall
Progressive Disclosure
Visual Hierarchy
Consistency and Standards
Efficient Search & Filtering
Cognitive Load Reduction
Outcome

The redesigned experience made navigating large code repositories faster and more intuitive.

Key improvements included:

Faster retrieval of code files through improved search and filtering.
Reduced effort in identifying the correct version using visible metadata.
Improved efficiency when working with large repositories.
Better organization and discoverability of stored code.
Streamlined workflows for developers and QA teams.
Reflection

This project demonstrated that managing technical assets is fundamentally an information architecture challenge. By prioritizing search efficiency, metadata visibility, and intuitive navigation, the design transformed a repository of code into a system that helped users find the right information quickly and confidently, supporting faster development workflows.
